"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][2]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – Vectors, matrices and data tensors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M1; school-level algebra.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model data as arrays, interpret\ntheir shapes and calculate a linear transformation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a measurement record to a matrix<\/h2>\n<p>A laboratory measures two dimensionless, already normalised sensor\nvalues for each of three samples. A single observation can be written as\na column vector, and several observations as a matrix. We define each\nrow as an observation and each column as a sensor. All numbers in this\nmodule are original synthetic examples.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad X=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\4&amp;3\\\\1&amp;5\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The vector has two components and the matrix has shape\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn general,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans that there are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns with real-valued entries. An index pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nidentifies the value in row\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand column\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin mathematical notation we start at 1. Transposition exchanges the two\naxes.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore has shape\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Axes and tensor shapes<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001509_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A matrix with three samples and two sensors; four matrices at different times form an array of shape (3, 4, 2).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A matrix with three samples and two\nsensors; four matrices at different times form an array of shape (3, 4,\n2).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Data shapes and axes.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>If the same three samples are analysed at four time points, the\nresult is a three-dimensional array\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times4\\times2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere the axes represent sample, time point and sensor.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{2,3,1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to sensor 1 on sample 2 at the third time point. A matrix is a\nspecial case of a multidimensional array. In data science such an array\nis often called a tensor. The stricter mathematical notion of a tensor\nalso includes specified transformation properties; these are not assumed\nhere.<\/p>\n<p>Shape is not a complete description: two arrays can have the same\nsize but different meanings for their axes. Swapping the time and sensor\naxes can produce computationally valid but scientifically incorrect\nresults. NumPy uses zero-based indices: the value mentioned above is\nfound at <code>T[1, 2, 0]<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linear combinations of features<\/h2>\n<p>For a simple indicator, the first sensor receives weight 2 and the\nsecond weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(2,-1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe calculate all three indicators at once:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Xw=\\begin{pmatrix}4-1\\\\8-3\\\\2-5\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\5\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a matrix product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n\\times d)(d\\times k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe inner dimensions must match. The result has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns. This is not element-wise multiplication. The weights may later\nbe learnt; here they are specified in advance. For raw values with\ndifferent units, the weights and scaling must be justified in the\napplication domain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Xv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(1,2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is the shape of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^TX<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nHow many entries does the array\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove contain?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Xv=(4,10,11)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^TX<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas shape\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause the shared dimension 3 is contracted.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot4\\cdot2=24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries. The next lesson explains how to compare two feature\nvectors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://numpy.org/doc/2.3/user/absolute_beginners.html\">NumPy 2.3,\nNumPy: the absolute basics for beginners, Array attributes\n(documentation; search excerpt).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending; access date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20);\nsubstantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://symm4ml.mit.edu/symm4ml_s26/notes/linear-algebra\">MIT\nSymm4ML, Linear Algebra for Representations, sections 1–5 and 10–11\n(online course notes).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Dot products, norms and distances<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate lengths, Euclidean\ndistances and cosine similarity, and assess their dependence on\nscale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing two measurement profiles<\/h2>\n<p>Let two normalised measurement profiles be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(3,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(1,3)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA comparison may ask how much the values differ, or whether both\nprofiles point in a similar direction. These questions are not\nequivalent: a vector twice as large has the same direction but may be\nfar away.<\/p>\n<p>For real vectors of the same length, the dot product is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^Tb=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}a_ib_i=3\\cdot1+1\\cdot3=6.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It produces a single number. Orthogonality means a dot product of\nzero. However, the sign alone is not sufficient to assess the degree of\nsimilarity: the lengths of the vectors also influence the result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Norms measure magnitude<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001730_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The straight line between (3, 1) and (1, 3) has length √8; the axis-aligned path has length 4.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The straight line between (3, 1) and (1,\n3) has length √8; the axis-aligned path has length 4.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two distances between measurement profiles.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnorm adds absolute values, the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnorm calculates geometric length, and the maximum norm considers the\ncomponent with the greatest absolute value:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|x\\|_1=\\sum_i|x_i|,\\qquad\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt{\\sum_i x_i^2},\\qquad\\|x\\|_\\infty=\\max_i|x_i|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe results are 4,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 3. The distance between two vectors associated with the respective\nnorm is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_p(a,b)=\\|a-b\\|_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a-b=(2,-2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_2=\\sqrt{8}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_\\infty=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEuclidean distance is the length of the straight line joining the\npoints, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the sum of the horizontal and vertical changes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Direction and scaling<\/h2>\n<p>The cosine of the angle between two non-zero real vectors is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>6<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(a,b)=\\frac{a^Tb}{\\|a\\|_2\\|b\\|_2}=\\frac{6}{10}=0.6.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Cosine similarity ranges from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto 1. It is undefined for a zero vector. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is 1, although their Euclidean distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAccordingly, it is useful when the direction of a feature profile\nmatters more than its overall magnitude. Unlike Euclidean distance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally not a metric.<\/p>\n<p>Changing the unit of measurement of a single axis changes distances\nand often angles as well. For heterogeneous features, standardisation\nusing the training mean and training standard deviation can help. It\ndoes not replace domain-specific weighting: some features may be more\nimportant than others. Features with no variation require special\ntreatment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(2,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(0,4)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(4,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalculate the cosine similarities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(u,v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(u,w)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the two Euclidean distances.<\/p>\n<p>Solution: the dot products are 0 and 8, giving similarities of 0 and\n1. The distances are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>20<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{20}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 2, respectively. When searching for similar data points, the\ncriterion for defining similarity must therefore be established first.\nNext, the dot product will also enable geometric projections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://symm4ml.mit.edu/symm4ml_s26/notes/linear-algebra\">MIT\nSymm4ML, Linear Algebra for Representations, sections 1–5 and 10–11\n(online course notes).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – Systems of linear equations and projections<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish exact and\noverdetermined linear systems and derive an orthogonal projection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Determining unknowns from measurements<\/h2>\n<p>A simple calibration model describes two unknown parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing two equations:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Ax=b,\\quad A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad b=\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\8\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Subtracting the first equation from the second gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe solution is unique because the columns of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare linearly independent. For square invertible matrices,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=A^{-1}b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexists in theory. Numerically, the system is usually solved directly\nwithout computing the entire inverse.<\/p>\n<p>When there are more measurement equations than parameters, an exact\nsolution is not guaranteed. Noise may cause the measured values to lie\noutside the column space of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe then seek a value that keeps the discrepancies small.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Projection onto a line<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001661_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The measurement vector (2, 4) decomposes into the projection (3, 3) and the perpendicular residual (−1, 1).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The measurement vector (2, 4) decomposes\ninto the projection (3, 3) and the perpendicular residual (−1,\n1).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Orthogonal projection onto a line.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A one-dimensional model is to approximate the measurement vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(2,4)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby a multiple of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(1,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe closest point on the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{tu:t\\in\\mathbb R\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the orthogonal projection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residual vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=y-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis perpendicular to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^T(y-tu)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolving gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>6<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{u^Ty}{u^Tu}=\\frac{6}{2}=3,\\qquad p=(3,3)^T,\\qquad r=(-1,1)^T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Orthogonality can be checked directly:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^Tr=-1+1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe distance to the line is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe solution exists and is unique because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=p+r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the right angle between the line and the residual vector.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Least squares for several parameters<\/h2>\n<p>For a general real-valued system we consider the minimisation\nproblem<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_x\\|Ax-b\\|_2^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At the minimum, the residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b-Ax<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis orthogonal to each column of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives the normal equations<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^TAx=A^Tb.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas full column rank, the solution is unique. Even with rank deficiency,\na best approximating vector exists in the column space, but several\nparameter vectors can produce the same vector. In numerical methods, QR\nor singular value decomposition is often more suitable than explicitly\nforming\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^TA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nsquaring the singular values worsens the conditioning of an\nill-conditioned problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Project\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(4,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonto the line with direction vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(2,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the residual as well.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=(8+1)/(4+1)=9/5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(18/5,9/5)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=(2/5,-4/5)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe check\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^Tr=4/5-4/5=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconfirms the right angle. In later regression lessons this geometric\nidea becomes a method for parameter estimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://symm4ml.mit.edu/symm4ml_s26/notes/linear-algebra\">MIT\nSymm4ML, Linear Algebra for Representations, sections 1–5 and 10–11\n(online course notes).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Eigenvalues, singular values and matrix factorisations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish eigendirections,\nsingular value decomposition and rank reduction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Directions of a linear transformation<\/h2>\n<p>A square matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan rotate, stretch, compress or redirect a vector. An eigenvector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines a direction whose line is preserved by the transformation\n(possibly collapsed to zero):<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Av=\\lambda v.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called an eigenvalue. A negative eigenvalue reverses the orientation;\na zero eigenvalue maps the vector to zero. Consider the real matrix<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1,1)^T=3(1,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(1,-1)^T=(1,-1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalues are 3 and 1. In general, eigenvalues are obtained as\nsolutions of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(over the complex numbers if necessary). A real symmetric matrix has an\northonormal eigenbasis and can be written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=Q\\Lambda Q^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Singular values for rectangular matrices too<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001702_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The matrix diag(4, 1) stretches the unit circle into an ellipse with semi-axes four and one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The matrix diag(4, 1) stretches the unit\ncircle into an ellipse with semi-axes four and one.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Linear stretching of the unit circle.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A rectangular data matrix cannot have an eigenvalue decomposition in\nthe sense described above. Singular value decomposition (SVD), however,\nexists for every real matrix. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=U\\Sigma V^T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare orthogonal matrices of sizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\times n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rectangular diagonal matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas non-negative singular values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2\\ge\\dots\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey are the square roots of the corresponding eigenvalues of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^TB<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(with only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min(m,n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsingular values, including zeros where appropriate). The normalised\nright singular vectors specify input directions, while the left singular\nvectors specify the corresponding output directions for positive\nsingular values.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\mathrm{diag}(4,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nboth changes of basis are the identity and the singular values are 4 and\n1. The unit circle becomes an ellipse with semi-axes 4 and 1. The\ndiagram illustrates this transformation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Factorisation and data compression<\/h2>\n<p>Matrices can be factored for different tasks. QR decomposes a matrix\ninto orthonormal directions and an upper triangular factor; LU uses\ntriangular factors (with permutation if necessary). An SVD can be used\nfor rank reduction:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_k=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sigma_i u_i v_i^T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If we retain only the largest singular value in the example, we\nobtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_1=\\mathrm{diag}(4,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error in the Frobenius norm is 1. In general, this truncation gives\na best\nrank-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximation in the Frobenius norm. However, small singular values are\nnot automatically unimportant in the application domain. A rare but\nrelevant data structure may have little total energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\mathrm{diag}(-5,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndetermine the eigenvalues and singular values. What is the error in the\nFrobenius norm of a rank-1 version obtained by truncating the SVD?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: the eigenvalues are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 2;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^TC=\\mathrm{diag}(25,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the singular values are 5 and 2. The error is 2. Eigenvalues may be\nnegative, but singular values cannot. This distinction is needed later\nfor principal component analysis and numerical stability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://symm4ml.mit.edu/symm4ml_s26/notes/linear-algebra\">MIT\nSymm4ML, Linear Algebra for Representations, sections 1–5 and 10–11\n(online course notes).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06sc-linear-algebra-fall-2011/pages/positive-definite-matrices-and-applications/singular-value-decomposition/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, Linear Algebra 18.06SC, Singular Value Decomposition\n(session overview).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access\ndate recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive check\nnot evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Derivatives, gradients and Jacobian matrices<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>;\nelementary differentiation rules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret partial derivatives\nand the Jacobian matrix, and estimate local changes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Changes in a model function<\/h2>\n<p>A model has several adjustable parameters. How sensitive is its\noutput to changes in these parameters? The derivative of a scalar\nfunction of one variable is the limit of the difference quotient, if it\nexists:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lim<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}h.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(x)=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor a small positive step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe function value changes by approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a local statement, not an exact value for arbitrary step\nsizes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Partial derivatives and the gradient<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001587_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptical contour lines of x²+2y²; at (1, 1), the gradient and negative gradient are normal to the contour line.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptical contour lines of x²+2y²; at\n(1, 1), the gradient and negative gradient are normal to the contour\nline.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gradient and contour lines.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x,y)=x^2+2y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe examine one variable at a time while holding the other fixed. The\npartial derivatives are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial g/\\partial x=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial g/\\partial y=4y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether they form the column gradient<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla g(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}2x\\\\4y\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a small step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(0.1,-0.1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nassuming differentiability, we have approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g\\approx\\nabla g^Th=-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe exact new value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(1.1,0.9)=2.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.17<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow the initial value of 3. The approximation neglects curvature. With\nthe Euclidean norm, the gradient, provided it is non-zero, points in the\ndirection of steepest local ascent; the negative gradient points in the\nopposite direction. The diagram marks ascent and descent perpendicular\nto the contour line.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Several outputs: the Jacobian matrix<\/h2>\n<p>For a vector-valued function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe derivatives are arranged in a matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts rows represent outputs, and its columns represent inputs. For\nexample,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>y<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>x<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}xy\\\\x+2y\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad J_F(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}y&amp;x\\\\1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces the change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.3,0.1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this special case the linear approximation is exact because only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges. For composed differentiable functions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\circ F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe chain rule is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_{G\\circ F}(x)=J_G(F(x))J_F(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe dimensions determine the order. Many methods for computing\nderivatives in neural networks are also based on this rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the gradient of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x,y)=3x^2+xy<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the approximate change for the step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0.1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla q=(6x+y,x)^T=(13,2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe linear change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhere it is exact. For non-differentiable functions, such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat zero, such a classical derivative is not available. The next lesson\nalso examines changes in the gradient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-s096-matrix-calculus-for-machine-learning-and-beyond-january-iap-2023/pages/lecture-notes-and-readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 18.S096 Matrix Calculus for Machine Learning and Beyond,\nLecture Notes and Readings (HTML overview).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M2-L6 – Multivariate functions, the Hessian matrix and Taylor approximation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe curvature using the\nHessian matrix and apply a local quadratic approximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why slope alone is not enough<\/h2>\n<p>For a differentiable function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mo>→<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe gradient describes the local slope. At a stationary point,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not yet imply a minimum: a maximum or saddle point can have\nthe same property.<\/p>\n<p>The Hessian matrix collects the second partial derivatives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{ij}(x)=\\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j}(x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If these derivatives are continuous in a neighbourhood,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis symmetric. Along the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+tv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith fixed direction vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe second derivative at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^TH(x)v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude and sign may vary with direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three types of curvature<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001592_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Cross-sections along the axes for x²+y², −x²−y² and x²−y²: two matching or opposite curvature directions, respectively.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Cross-sections along the axes for x²+y²,\n−x²−y² and x²−y²: two matching or opposite curvature directions,\nrespectively.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Curvature at stationary points.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider the functions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=x^2+y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_2=-x^2-y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_3=x^2-y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll three have a zero gradient at the origin. The Hessian matrices are\ndiagonal, with diagonal entries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hessian matrix at the stationary point<\/th>\n<th>Conclusion<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Positive definite<\/td>\n<td>Strict local minimum<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative definite<\/td>\n<td>Strict local maximum<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indefinite<\/td>\n<td>Saddle point<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These criteria apply under sufficient smoothness; if the matrix is\nmerely semidefinite, classification may remain inconclusive. For\nexample,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-x^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nboth have a second derivative of zero at the origin but different types\nof extrema. The diagram shows cross-sections of the three\ntwo-dimensional functions along the coordinate axes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Second-order Taylor approximation<\/h2>\n<p>For a twice continuously differentiable function, in a neighbourhood\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a+h)=f(a)+\\nabla f(a)^Th+\\tfrac12 h^TH(a)h+o(\\|h\\|_2^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The remainder notation means that the remainder divided by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|h\\|_2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)=x^2+xy+2y^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(1,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f(a)=(3,5)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(0.2,-0.1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe linear contribution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the quadratic contribution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.04<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe new function value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4.14<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor this quadratic function the remainder is exactly zero. For general\nfunctions the approximation is reliable only locally; a large step may\nmisrepresent the curvature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Determine the gradient and Hessian matrix of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x,y)=x^2+4y^2-2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhere is the stationary point, and how can it be classified?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla q=(2x-2,8y)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe gradient vanishes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\mathrm{diag}(2,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive definite. The point is a strict local minimum, and in this\ncase also a global minimum, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(x-1)^2+4y^2-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis analysis forms the basis of curvature-based optimisation\nmethods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-s096-matrix-calculus-for-machine-learning-and-beyond-january-iap-2023/pages/lecture-notes-and-readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 18.S096 Matrix Calculus for Machine Learning and Beyond,\nLecture Notes and Readings (HTML overview).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M2-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M2-L7 – Numerical stability and floating-point arithmetic<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics for Data-Driven Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish rounding,\ncancellation, conditioning and stability, and formulate a more stable\ncalculation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Real numbers and their machine representation<\/h2>\n<p>A computer stores real numbers only approximately. In the widely used\nIEEE 754 binary64 format, 53 significant binary digits are available for\nnormalised numbers (including the implicit leading digit). An exponent\nis stored in addition to the sign. Because the available number space is\nfinite, many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly.<\/p>\n<p>The gap between 1 and the next larger binary64 number is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>52<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{-52}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen rounding to nearest, the relative rounding error bound for\nnormalised results is typically at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>53<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=2^{-53}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided overflow or underflow does not violate the assumptions of the\nmodel. The absolute gap between adjacent numbers is not uniform.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cancellation and rearranged formulae<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001586_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Direct subtraction gives zero in binary64; the rationalised formula gives approximately 5 times 10 to the power of −9.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Direct subtraction gives zero in\nbinary64; the rationalised formula gives approximately 5 times 10 to the\npower of −9.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Cancellation with nearly equal numbers.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In binary64 with rounding to nearest, let us calculate the following\nexpression at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=10^8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\sqrt{x^2+1}-x.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The square is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>16<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{16}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthere the gap to the next representable number is 2. Adding 1 in the\nusual rounding mode (ties to even) rounds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>16<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{16}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two terms of the subsequent subtraction therefore appear identical,\nand the numerical result is zero. Mathematically,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive. Rationalising gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}+x}\\approx5\\cdot10^{-9}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The result can now be well approximated even in binary64. In this\ncase the rearrangement avoids subtracting almost identical numbers. For\nextremely large magnitudes, possible overflow when forming the square\nmust also be prevented. The diagram compares the two calculation\npaths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Problem conditioning and algorithm stability<\/h2>\n<p>Conditioning describes how strongly the mathematical solution changes\nwhen the inputs are perturbed slightly. For an invertible matrix, with\nrespect to the Euclidean norm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_2(A)=\\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf this ratio is large, small errors in the data can be greatly\namplified. For example, the matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{diag}(1,10^{-6})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas condition number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Stability, by contrast, is a property of a specific computational\nprocedure. A backward stable algorithm delivers approximately an exact\nsolution to a slightly perturbed problem. Even then, poor conditioning\ncan lead to a large forward error. The concepts are therefore not\ninterchangeable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Compare the two calculation paths in Python:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> math<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">100_000_000.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>root <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> math.sqrt(x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(root <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> (root <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> x))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Solution on a typical binary64 system: <code>0.0<\/code> and\n<code>5e-09<\/code>. Check approximate values using absolute and relative\ntolerances chosen for the application rather than blindly testing exact\nequality. Such tests must take the scale of the problem into account.\nThe module thus ends with an important limitation of numerical models:\ncorrect mathematics alone does not guarantee a reliable\nimplementation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/floatingpoint.html\">Python\n3.14, Floating-Point Arithmetic: Issues and Limitations, ch. 15\n(documentation).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access\ndate recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive check\nnot evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://symm4ml.mit.edu/symm4ml_s26/notes/linear-algebra\">MIT\nSymm4ML, Linear Algebra for Representations, sections 1–5 and 10–11\n(online course notes).<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
